ENGINEERING MANDATE & FORMATTING PROTOCOL
ABSOLUTE DIRECTIVES
COMPLIANCE MANDATORY: All AI assistance must strictly adhere to the requirements outlined in this document. No deviations, simplifications, or compromises are permitted.
ENGINEERING GRADE SOLUTIONS: All implementations, algorithms, and solutions must meet industrial simulation standards. Accuracy, stability, and predictive validity are non-negotiable across all operating conditions.
REALISM ABSOLUTE: Under no circumstances shall realism be compromised for simplified gameplay, ease of implementation, or non-physical approximations. All systems must adhere strictly to verifiable physical and engineering principles.

CODE FORMATTING STANDARDS
Header File (.h) Function Declarations
REQUIRED FORMAT: Single-line compact style with inline documentation
cpp/** Vehicle preset initialization */
void initializeDefaultVehiclePreset(int32 PresetID);
PROHIBITED FORMAT: Multi-line verbose doxygen style
cpp// DO NOT USE THIS FORMAT
/**
 * @brief Computes longitudinal tire force
 * @param Member The axle member
 * @return Longitudinal force
 */
float ComputeLongitudinalForce(const FAxleMember& Member, float SlipRatio);
RULE: Function signature and all arguments on ONE line. Units shown in comment, not in parameter documentation.

Variable Documentation - Physics Units
REQUIRED FORMAT: Inline unit notation with proper mathematical symbols
cppFVector TotalLateralForce;  // [N] - Total lateral acceleration force
float Bx = Kx / (Cx * (Dx + 1e-3f));  // [N⁻¹] (kg⁻¹⋅m⁻¹⋅s²)
REQUIREMENTS:

Use proper mathematical superscripts/subscripts (⁻¹, ⋅, ², etc.)
Include derived unit breakdowns in parentheses when helpful
Keep units physically accurate and dimensionally consistent
Place unit comment on same line as variable declaration


Conditional Statements - Reasoning & Closure
REQUIRED FORMAT: Reason stated at opening, closure identification at end
cpp// Check if suspension torque exists before applying
if (SuspensionConfig.TotalTorqueRad.SizeSquared() > UE_SMALL_NUMBER)
{
    VehicleHull->AddTorqueInRadians(SuspensionConfig.TotalTorqueRad);
} // End if (TotalTorqueRad check)
REQUIREMENTS:

Comment explaining the logical reason BEFORE the if statement
Closing comment identifying which conditional is ending
Nested conditionals must clearly identify their closure points


Code Density - Single Line Logic
PREFERRED: Compact single-line statements
cppDrawDebugLine(World, WheelWorldLocation, WheelWorldLocation + VelScale, FColor::White, false, -1.0f, 0, 2.0f);
AVOID: Unnecessarily verbose multi-line formatting
cpp// DO NOT USE THIS STYLE
if (GEngine)
{
    GEngine->AddOnScreenDebugMessage(
        1, 1.5f, FColor::Yellow,
        FString::Printf(TEXT("Throttle: %.3f"), Result)
    );
}
RULE: If logic fits reasonably on one line, keep it on one line. Prioritize code density without sacrificing readability.

Function Naming Convention
REQUIREMENTS:

Technical, short, and catchy
NO generic internet-sourced names
NO Greek-themed terminology
NO sci-fi references
NO geeky/nerdy/lame conventions
NO space-themed naming
NO overly scientific jargon

ACCEPTABLE STYLE: Precise engineering terminology that is memorable and distinctive

Section Dividers
MAJOR SECTION FORMAT:
cpp//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//                                    suspension pipeline
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
COMMENT BLOCK FORMAT:
cpp//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Tire slip calculation
// Computing longitudinal and lateral slip ratios
// Using modified Pacejka coefficients
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REQUIREMENTS:

78-character width for divider lines
Section titles centered or left-aligned as appropriate
Comment blocks use same divider style for visual consistency


PERFORMANCE & CODE QUALITY MANDATES
PERFORMANCE PRIORITY: All code must be optimized for maximum speed and minimal computational overhead. Performance is a top-level design constraint.
CODE PRACTICE PROHIBITION: Strictly forbidden from utilizing, mimicking, or copying poor coding practices, legacy implementations, or substandard code structures. Readability, maintainability, and efficiency are paramount.
TELEMETRY PROTOCOL: CSV logging is mandatory for performance-critical telemetry data capture to bypass overhead of standard logging systems.

CONFLICT RESOLUTION PROTOCOL
PHYSICS DEVIATION WARNING: If any directive or assumption violates established vehicle dynamics, thermodynamics, or engineering principles, AI SHALL immediately halt and issue explicit warning.
WARNING CONTENT MUST INCLUDE:

Physical principle being compromised
Potential adverse effects on realism or stability
Correct engineering principle that should be followed

CONFIRMATION REQUIRED: NO CHANGE will be made until explicit user confirmation is received acknowledging the compromise.

